\viewkind4\pard\cf1\fs32\b Understand what Wake-on-LAN can prove\b0\fs22\par\par
\pard\sa120 Wake-on-LAN sends a magic-packet pattern for a specific network adapter's MAC address. The target must already support and be configured for wake-up; the application cannot enable firmware or operating-system power settings remotely.\par
\pard\li240\ri240\sa120\f1 Magic payload: six FF bytes, followed by the target MAC repeated 16 times.\par
Each MAC is 6 bytes.\par
Payload length = 6 + 16 \u215? 6 = 102 bytes.\f0\par
\pard\sa120 Choose the destination address, sending interface and UDP port explicitly. The proposed default port is 9. Broadcast forwarding and wake behaviour depend on the network and device configuration; do not assume the packet can cross a router.\par
\pard\sa120 A successful send means Windows accepted the datagram for sending. It does not acknowledge receipt or prove the PC woke up. A follow-up ping or connection check is a separate action.\par
\pard\sa120 \cf2\ul Microsoft \u8212? UDP sendto behaviour\ul0\cf1  (online: \cf2\ul https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsock2/nf-winsock2-sendto\ul0\cf1 )\par
